{"card":{"card_id":"ma-c-b77f19806945451b6848","statement":"It was established in <<cite:BGV>> that the Spaltenstein basis as above coincides with the Mirkovi\\' c-Vilonen basis of <<cite:MV>>. As far as we know the question about the relationship between the semicanonical (as well as Lusztig's canonical <<cite:L90, L91>>) and Mirkovi\\' c-Vilonen bases remains open.\n\n\\sus{On normal (transverse) slices}\n\nLet $\\fg=\\gl(D)$ and $G = GL(D)$.\n\n\\sss{ Normal slices to nilpotent orbits }\n\nWe will say that a normal slice (in $\\fg$) to a nilpotent orbit $\\al$ at $e\\in\\al$, is a submanifold $S$ of $\\fg$ such that\n\n- (Infinitesimal normality.) $T_e\\al\\ \\pl\\  T_eS=\\ \\fg$, (cf. <<cite:CG>>) and\n\n- (Contraction.) There is an action of $G_m$ on $S$ which contracts it to $e$ and preserves intersections with the Lusztig strata in $\\fg$. (For the definition of Lusztig strata cf. <<cite:Mir>> and references therein.)\n\nWe will use the terminology \"normal slice\" and \"transverse slice\" interchangeably.\n\n\\lemm For a normal slice $S$\n\n- $S\\cap \\al=\\{e\\}$.\n\n- $S$ meets Lusztig stratum $\\be$ iff $\\al\\sub \\barr\\be$.\n\n- $S$ meets Lusztig strata transversally.\n\n\\lemm A sufficient data for a normal slice to the orbit $\\ ^Ge$ at $e$ is given by a pair $(h,C)$ where $h\\in\\fg$ is semisimple integral (i.e., eigenvalues of $\\ad\\ h$ are integral), and $[h,e]=2e$; while $C\\sub \\fg$ is an $h$-invariant vector subspace complementary to $T_e(\\al)=[\\fg,e]$, such that the eigenvalues of $h$ in $C$ are ${\\le 1}$. Then $S=e+C$ is a normal slice.","source_id":"arXiv:0712.4160","cluster":"algebraic_geometry","statement_sha256":"63c565cf815b70a8f24229d2068ce9b4e3ffe97f40020e63e8a7b5b12ba9c7f5","kind":"remark","label":"Remark 2","ref_key":"","name":"","order":35,"deps":[],"cite_keys":["BGV","CG","L90","L91","MV","Mir"],"provenance":{"source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0712.4160","harvest_mode":"arxiv_eprint","payload_sha256":"2278c1ffa45bc8c23d37808ce89ce2cad02ffa7ed7597e7682625d1d99195de2","line":1177,"origin":"latex_heading","retrieved_at":"2026-09-27T21:50:46+08:00"},"wash":{"version":"cardwash.v3","stages":["segment","purify","classify","dedupe","emit"],"numbering":"document_order_with_label_heuristic","detail":{"stripped_proof":true,"chars":1464,"math_hints":100}}},"paper":{"source_id":"arXiv:0712.4160","title":"Quiver varieties and Beilinson-Drinfeld Grassmannians of type A","abstract":"We construct Nakajima's quiver varieties of type A in terms of conjugacy classes of matrices and (non-Slodowy's) transverse slices naturally arising from affine Grassmannians. In full generality quiver varieties are embedded into Beilinson-Drinfeld Grassmannians of type A. Our construction provides a compactification of Nakajima's quiver varieties and a decomposition of an affine Grassmannian into a disjoint union of quiver varieties. As an application we provide a geometric version of skew and symmetric $(GL(m), GL(n))$ duality.","authors":["Ivan Mirković","Maxim Vybornov"],"year":"2007","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0712.4160","cluster":"algebraic_geometry","macros":{"ra":"{      \\rightarrow     }","mm":"{\\mapsto}","barr":"\\overline","sub":"\\subseteq","se":"\\section","sus":"\\subsection","sss":"\\subsubsection","lemm":"\\subsubsection{Lemma}","slemm":"\\subsubsection*{Lemma}","scorr":"\\subsubsection*{\\bf Corollary}","Theo":"\\subsection{Theorem}","theo":"\\subsubsection{\\rm{\\bf Theorem}}","stheo":"\\subsubsection*{\\rm{\\bf Theorem}}","pl":"{\\oplus}","cd":"{\\cdot}","cddd":"{\\cdots}","Im":"{      \\operatorname{Im}       }","Hom":"{    \\operatorname{Hom}      }","End":"{     \\operatorname{End}      }","AA":"{\\mathcal A}","FF":"{\\mathcal F}","GG":"{\\mathcal G}","HH":"{\\mathcal H}","KK":"{\\mathcal K}","LL":"{\\mathcal L}","NN":"{\\mathcal N}","OO":"{\\mathcal O}","PP":"{\\mathcal P}","RR":"{\\mathcal R}","TT":"\\widetilde T","UU":"{\\mathcal U}","VV":"{\\mathcal V}","A":"{\\mathbb A }","C":"{\\mathbb C}","